'''''Rhododendron simsii''''' (杜鵑) is a [[rhododendron]] [[species]] native to [[East Asia]], where it grows at altitudes of {{convert|500-2700|m|ft|abbr=on}}.

==Description==
[[File:Rhododendron indicum - Kunming Botanical Garden - DSC02873.JPG|thumb|left|''Rhododendron simsii'' in [[Kunming Botanical Garden]]]]
It is a twiggy [[evergreen]] or semi-evergreen shrub growing to {{convert|2|m|ft|abbr=on}} in height, with leaves that are ovate, elliptic-ovate or obovate to [[oblanceolate]], 1.5–5 by 0.5–3&nbsp;cm in size. The flowers range from white to dark red. Some varieties of '''''Rhododendron simsii''''' are poisonous due to the presence of [[grayanotoxin]].<ref>Poon WT, Ho CH, Yip KL, Lai CK, Cheung KL, Sung RY, Chan AY, Mak TW [https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/18840915 Grayanotoxin poisoning from Rhododendron simsii in an infant.] Hong Kong Med J. 2008 Oct;14(5):405-7</ref>

==Distribution==
The species is common in Hong Kong. It is also distributed in Areas south of [[Yangtze]] in China as well as in Vietnam and Thailand.<ref>[[Hong Kong Herbarium]] [http://www.hkherbarium.net/Herbarium/PDF/Leaflet%2013_LR.pdf Native Azaleas of Hong Kong]</ref>

The specific name ''simsii'' commemorates [[John Sims (taxonomist)|John Sims]] (1749-1831) who was the first editor of "Magazine Botanique".
<ref>{{in lang|fr}} [http://www.rhododendron.fr/articles/dico_s.pdf Dictionnaire étymologique de botanique]</ref>

This slightly tender species is quite rare in the west, though well known in Chinese gardens.<ref name = TSO>{{cite web | url = https://treesandshrubsonline.org/articles/rhododendron/rhododendron-simsii/ | title = ''Rhododendron simsii'' | website = Trees and Shrubs Online | publisher = International Dendrology Society  | access-date = 22 April 2021}}</ref>

